## A Year Dominated by Recalls

With a staggering 759 recalls this year alone, the food industry seems to be struggling with safety protocols. The majority of these cases fall under Class II recalls (457 incidents), with a substantial number of Class I recalls (206 incidents) indicating high-risk safety issues. It's clear that there's an urgent need for improved practices.

## Repeat Offenders and Worrisome Trends

Among the top brands with the highest number of recalls, Spring & Mulberry Inc. stands out for all the wrong reasons. With 11 recalls this year, all classified as Class I, the brand's issues with Salmonella contamination in their date-sweetened chocolates highlight serious lapses in quality control.

## Recent Recalls of Note

Here are some of the most concerning recalls reported in early July:

1. **Spring & Mulberry Inc.** - Date-sweetened chocolates contaminated with Salmonella.
2. **Utz Quality Foods, LLC** - Multiple flavors of chips potentially contaminated with Salmonella.
3. **George J Howe Company, Inc.** - Undeclared tree nut allergen in sunflower seeds.

These issues mostly stem from either contamination during processing or mislabeling, both of which are preventable with tighter regulatory oversight and compliance.

## Moving Forward

While the number of recalls shows that issues are being identified and addressed, it also underscores significant gaps in food safety that need immediate attention. Brands with repeated offenses need to be held accountable, and consumers should remain informed to make safer choices.
